<%= lato_page_head "Documentation" %>

<h2>How to use Lato Storage</h2>

<p>
  <code>lato_storage</code> adds an Active Storage management section to a Lato admin panel.
  Use it to monitor uploaded files, inspect storage usage, and run cleanup operations from the back office.
</p>

<h3>Installation</h3>

<p>Requires Rails 8.1.3.1 or newer in the 8.1 series, Lato 3.25.0 or newer and Active Storage installed (<code>rails active_storage:install</code>); libvips 8.13 or newer if you use variants.
Existing applications must run <code>rails lato:install:migrations</code> and <code>rails db:migrate</code>
before deployment. Previous sessions are invalidated.</p>

<p>Add Lato Storage to the application Gemfile:</p>

<pre><code>gem "lato"
gem "lato_storage"</code></pre>

<p>Install the engine and run migrations:</p>

<pre><code>bundle
rails lato_storage:install:migrations
rails db:migrate</code></pre>

<p>Mount the engine in <code>config/routes.rb</code>:</p>

<pre><code>Rails.application.routes.draw do
  mount LatoStorage::Engine =&gt; "/lato-storage"

  # ...
end</code></pre>

<p>Import styles in <code>app/assets/stylesheets/application.scss</code>:</p>

<pre><code>@import "lato_storage/application";</code></pre>

<p>Import JavaScript in <code>app/javascript/application.js</code>:</p>

<pre><code>import "lato_storage/application"</code></pre>

<p>Add a menu entry in the host <code>layouts/lato/_navbar-nav_content</code> or <code>_sidebar-nav_content</code> partials:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;% if @session.user&amp;.lato_storage_admin %&gt;
  &lt;%= lato_sidebar_nav_item :lato_storage, lato_storage.root_path do %&gt;
    Storage
  &lt;% end %&gt;
&lt;% end %&gt;</code></pre>

<h3>Permissions</h3>

<p>
  Users must be logged into Lato and must have Lato Storage admin permission enabled.
</p>

<div class="alert alert-warning">
  <p class="mb-0">
    The permission grants visibility on the files of <strong>all</strong> models, users and groups. Give it only to super admins.
    File links are signed, forced as download and expire after <code>blob_url_expires_in</code> (12 hours by default).
  </p>
</div>

<pre><code>user = Lato::User.find_by(email: "admin@example.com")
user.update!(lato_storage_admin: true)</code></pre>

<h3>What admins can do</h3>

<ul>
  <li>View an overview of storage usage.</li>
  <li>Check how many files, attachments, and variants exist.</li>
  <li>Browse uploaded files and related storage metadata.</li>
  <li>Identify unattached files that can be cleaned.</li>
  <li>Start a cleanup operation from the panel.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Performance configuration</h3>

<p>
  Large applications can enable performance optimization for the dashboard.
  When enabled, some dashboard numbers can be estimates instead of exact values.
</p>

<pre><code>LatoStorage.configure do |config|
  config.optimize_performances = true
  config.blob_url_expires_in = 12.hours
end</code></pre>

<p>With optimization enabled:</p>

<ul>
  <li>counts are estimates (table statistics on MySQL and PostgreSQL, exact count on other databases);</li>
  <li>total storage and content types are extrapolated from a sample of the last 5000 blobs;</li>
  <li>the largest blobs card is hidden and lists skip the total count;</li>
  <li>data is cached for 12 hours and the dashboard shows when it was last updated.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  Data is computed by <code>LatoStorage::PreloadDashboardDataJob</code>. It requires a <code>Rails.cache</code> store shared between
  web and worker processes and a real Active Job queue adapter. The first visit without cache computes the data synchronously;
  while a calculation is running the dashboard shows a "computing" notice. Pre-warm it on a schedule, for example with Solid Queue:
</p>

<pre><code>lato_storage_preload:
  class: LatoStorage::PreloadDashboardDataJob
  args: [true]
  schedule: every 6 hours</code></pre>

<div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
  <p class="mb-0">
    Keep optimization disabled when exact dashboard numbers are more important than speed.
    Enable it when storage tables become large and dashboard loading is slow.
  </p>
</div>

<h3>Cleanup</h3>

<p>
  The cleaner action runs as a Lato operation, so admins can follow progress and result in the operation page.
  Use it to remove files that are no longer attached to application records.
  It asks for confirmation and runs <code>purge_later</code> on every file without attachments created more than 12 hours ago,
  so an Active Job worker is required. Empty folders of a local disk service are removed too. Only one cleanup every 10 minutes can be started.
</p>

<div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
  <p class="mb-0">
    Deletion is irreversible. Before running cleanup in production, verify backups, storage retention rules, and application-specific file usage.
  </p>
</div>

<h3>Security</h3>

<p>
  Keep the Rails defaults for <code>content_types_to_serve_as_binary</code> and <code>content_types_allowed_inline</code>:
  user uploaded HTML or SVG files must never be served inline on the admin origin.
</p>
